{"title":"2026 CEMC Cayley Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Jin has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/7/78792fe4680a164f537ea72a4f900e4471433c3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> cupcakes, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e126f0d41c904d37ba2c332347a24b75d4ac3066.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$75\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> of which are chocolate, and the rest of which are vanilla. If Jin wants to bake more vanilla cupcakes so that half of the cupcakes are vanilla and half are chocolate, how many more vanilla cupcakes should Jin bake?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/e/e7e74a5329c2cf2c26c5f374ac8a370dfdc19b53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 10 \\qquad \t\\textbf{(B)}\\ 20  \\qquad \t\\textbf{(C)}\\ 25  \\qquad \t\\textbf{(D)}\\ 30  \\qquad \t\\textbf{(E)}\\ 40  \\qquad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"430\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Currently, there would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/d/bed25e2ea786da4f97e725299ea9ca8f52cc6c82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40 \\times 75\\% = 40 \\times \\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"153\" height=\"38\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/c/95ce9bb85b0d6d4cfcecc2fbd72afe506af3a8d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10 \\times 3 = 30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"94\" height=\"15\" /> chocolate cupcakes, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/7/b171dc177c7960c880aa604bf588015762595e65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40 - 30 = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"103\" height=\"15\" /> vanilla cupcakes.\n</p><p>For half of the cupcakes to be vanilla and half to be chocolate, the number of vanilla cupcakes would have to be the same as the number of chocolate cupcakes. This means that he would need to back <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/5/ae5a0f763973a459adaf921963c27eda13d93434.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30 - 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"60\" height=\"15\" /> more vanilla cupcakes, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/7/327e8815ea07db450f5ee8360b2e7d35b79abb36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf {(B) } 20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"66\" height=\"30\" />. \n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley\" title=\"2026 CEMC Cayley\">2026 CEMC Cayley</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Cayley Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Cayley_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Cayley Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">P